data: {"type":"handoff","handoff":{"text":"{\"v\":1,\"hash\":\"b9c4a57381ea9ddb699ee666755c58f30b5b91b61b304330fddf621f633a2b36\",\"at\":1794837045037,\"retried\":[],\"merged\":{\"byChunk\":[{\"chunkId\":1,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"1.1\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 (July 16–24, 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon and the fifth crewed mission of NASA's Apollo program.\"},{\"id\":\"1.2\",\"text\":\"The crew was Commander Neil Armstrong, Command Module Pilot Michael Collins, and Lunar Module Pilot Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin, all on their second and final spaceflight.\"},{\"id\":\"1.3\",\"text\":\"The Saturn V launched the spacecraft from Kennedy Space Center in Florida on July 16 at 13:32 UTC.\"},{\"id\":\"1.4\",\"text\":\"The service module provided propulsion, electrical power, oxygen, and water to the command module, which was the only part to return to Earth.\"},{\"id\":\"1.5\",\"text\":\"Armstrong and Aldrin descended aboard the LM Eagle and landed in the Sea of Tranquility on July 20 at 20:17 UTC, while Collins remained in lunar orbit aboard Columbia.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo program\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Command Module (CM)\",\"Service Module (SM)\",\"Lunar Module (LM)\"]},{\"chunkId\":2,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"2.1\",\"text\":\"Armstrong walked on the Moon about six hours after landing, with Aldrin following nineteen minutes later.\"},{\"id\":\"2.2\",\"text\":\"The two spent about two and a half hours on the surface, collecting 21.5 kg of lunar material and speaking with President Nixon.\"},{\"id\":\"2.3\",\"text\":\"The crew rejoined Collins in lunar orbit and returned to Earth, splashing down in the Pacific Ocean on July 24.\"},{\"id\":\"2.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 culminated the Space Race, a Cold War geopolitical competition between the United States and the Soviet Union.\"},{\"id\":\"2.5\",\"text\":\"President John F. Kennedy set the goal in May 1961 of landing a man on the Moon before the decade ended; the program overcame the fatal Apollo 1 fire in January 1967.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"Apollo 11\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Mercury\",\"Gemini\",\"Space Race\"]},{\"chunkId\":3,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"3.1\",\"text\":\"The Soviet Union could not match the Saturn V rocket, and its uncrewed probe Luna 15 crashed on the Moon on July 21 while Armstrong and Aldrin were on the surface.\"},{\"id\":\"3.2\",\"text\":\"Armstrong's moonwalk was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million viewers, about one-fifth of the world's population.\"},{\"id\":\"3.3\",\"text\":\"Stepping onto the lunar surface, Armstrong declared, \\\"That's one small step for [a] man, one giant leap for mankind.\\\"\"},{\"id\":\"3.4\",\"text\":\"Lunar samples led to the identification of three previously unrecognized minerals, and surface instruments returned data for years afterwards.\"},{\"id\":\"3.5\",\"text\":\"The crew received ticker-tape parades, a world goodwill tour, and the Presidential Medal of Freedom; Columbia is preserved at the National Air and Space Museum.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Luna 15\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Columbia\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Eagle\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"Cold War\"]},{\"chunkId\":4,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"4.1\",\"text\":\"On October 4, 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite, surprising the world and fueling fears about Soviet capabilities.\"},{\"id\":\"4.2\",\"text\":\"Sputnik's success suggested the USSR could potentially deliver nuclear weapons over intercontinental distances, challenging American claims of superiority.\"},{\"id\":\"4.3\",\"text\":\"Sputnik sparked the Sputnik crisis and ignited the Space Race between the two superpowers.\"},{\"id\":\"4.4\",\"text\":\"President Dwight D. Eisenhower responded by creating NASA and initiating Project Mercury, which aimed to place a man into Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"4.5\",\"text\":\"On April 12, 1961, Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space and first to orbit the Earth; on May 5, 1961, Alan Shepard became the first American in space on a suborbital flight.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"United States\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Space Race\"]},{\"chunkId\":5,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"5.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy, Eisenhower's successor, chose a challenge beyond existing rocket capabilities so both countries would start from an equal position.\"},{\"id\":\"5.2\",\"text\":\"A crewed Moon mission was presented as the challenge that would serve this purpose.\"},{\"id\":\"5.3\",\"text\":\"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy told Congress the nation should commit to landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the 1960s end.\"},{\"id\":\"5.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said the project would be more impressive and important than any other in the period, but also more difficult and expensive.\"},{\"id\":\"5.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed accelerating lunar spacecraft development, developing alternate liquid and solid fuel boosters, and funding engine development and unmanned explorations.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Earth\",\"Moon\"]},{\"chunkId\":6,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"6.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy says landing on the Moon will be an entire nation's effort, since everyone must work to put one man there.\"},{\"id\":\"6.2\",\"text\":\"On September 12, 1962, Kennedy gave a speech to about 40,000 people at Rice University stadium in Houston, Texas.\"},{\"id\":\"6.3\",\"text\":\"Kennedy argues outer space has no strife, prejudice, or national conflict yet, and its hazards threaten everyone alike.\"},{\"id\":\"6.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy says conquering space deserves the best of all mankind and offers a chance for peaceful cooperation that may not return.\"},{\"id\":\"6.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy answers the question of why go to the Moon by comparing it to climbing mountains, flying the Atlantic, and Rice playing Texas, ending with 'We choose to go to the Moon.'\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Moon\",\"Texas\",\"mankind\"]},{\"chunkId\":7,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"7.1\",\"text\":\"The speaker says the nation chooses to go to the Moon this decade because the hard goal will organize and measure its best energies and skills.\"},{\"id\":\"7.2\",\"text\":\"The proposed Moon program faced widespread opposition, and mathematician Norbert Wiener of MIT dubbed it a \\\"moondoggle.\\\"\"},{\"id\":\"7.3\",\"text\":\"The effort to land a man on the Moon was named Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"7.4\",\"text\":\"In June 1961, Kennedy proposed a joint Moon landing project to Khrushchev, who declined the offer.\"},{\"id\":\"7.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy again proposed a joint Moon expedition at the UN General Assembly on September 20, 1963; the idea was abandoned after his death.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"Soviet Union\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"Moon\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"Earth orbit rendezvous\"]},{\"chunkId\":8,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"8.1\",\"text\":\"In July 1962, NASA head James Webb announced that lunar orbit rendezvous would be used for the Apollo spacecraft.\"},{\"id\":\"8.2\",\"text\":\"The Apollo spacecraft had three parts: the command module, which alone returned to Earth; the service module; and the lunar module.\"},{\"id\":\"8.3\",\"text\":\"The service module provided propulsion, electrical power, oxygen, and water to support the command module.\"},{\"id\":\"8.4\",\"text\":\"The design allowed a single Saturn V rocket, then under development, to launch the spacecraft.\"},{\"id\":\"8.5\",\"text\":\"Apollo relied on Project Gemini for技术 and techniques, and on NASA's use of MOSFETs and silicon IC chips.\"}],\"entities\":[\"James Webb\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Saturn V\",\"Command module\",\"Service module\",\"Lunar module\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer\"]},{\"chunkId\":9,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"9.1\",\"text\":\"The Apollo 1 fire on January 27, 1967 killed astronauts Gus Grissom, Ed White, and Roger B. Chaffee, and halted Project Apollo abruptly.\"},{\"id\":\"9.2\",\"text\":\"In October 1968, Apollo 7 evaluated the command module in Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.3\",\"text\":\"In December 1968, Apollo 8 tested the command module in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.4\",\"text\":\"In March 1969, Apollo 9 tested the lunar module in Earth orbit; in May, Apollo 10 conducted a 'dress rehearsal' in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.5\",\"text\":\"By July 1969, all was in readiness for Apollo 11 to make the final step onto the Moon.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Project Apollo\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\",\"Apollo 11\"]}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo program\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Command Module (CM)\",\"Service Module (SM)\",\"Lunar Module (LM)\",\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Mercury\",\"Gemini\",\"Space Race\",\"Luna 15\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Columbia\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Eagle\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"Cold War\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Earth\",\"Moon\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Texas\",\"mankind\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"Earth orbit rendezvous\",\"James Webb\",\"Apollo\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Command module\",\"Service module\",\"Lunar module\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\"],\"findingCount\":9}}","seal":"d9326267e9d34efe6664f5db973ab91979510a1c395b1b65a55ebce929b03738"}}

